WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:07.140
We can train a language model with a pencil and a book. Our book is short enough to read in thirty seconds.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:19.960
Treat capitals and lowercase as the same word, even on printed sheets. "The" and "the" go in the same row. Full stops and exclamation marks count as words too.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:42.500
Now read it two words at a time. Add each new word to both edges of the grid. The first word gets a row. The second gets a column. Put a tally where they cross. Then slide along by one, and do it again.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:52.020
Keep going, and the same pairs come round again. By the end of the book, "the magpie" has five tallies. "The fence" has six.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:59.820
For our model, training means counting pairs. Here's the rest of the book, counted by a computer.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:08.300
We can put the book away now. The grid has counts for each pair of words, and that's what we'll use to write.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:19.600
And a tally is just a number. The row for "the" says five, six, four, four. Every box on the grid is a number, and most of them are zero.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:32.200
When people talk about a model's "weights", they mean numbers like these, set by the text it read. An LLM's weights aren't tallies of pairs, but they do the same job: predicting what comes next.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:41.620
We can count a bigger book the same way. This is Frankenstein. Eighty-five thousand words, and seven thousand rows.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:53.780
A large language model reads trillions of words, and it doesn't keep a box for every pair. But it's training for the same job: guessing what comes next. Yours fits on a desk.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:02:09.740
A few tips. Start on any page you like. A full stop is followed by whatever comes next, even over the page. Skip the speech marks. And a new word always gets a new row and a new column.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:17.180
It works best in pairs: one reads, one tallies, swap halfway. Start counting.
